1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,224
Early on, people treated getting good output as a wording problem.

2
00:00:04,224 --> 00:00:08,979
Phrase the instruction cleverly enough, add the right magic words, and the answer

3
00:00:08,979 --> 00:00:09,507
improves.

4
00:00:09,507 --> 00:00:14,379
That's prompt engineering, and it's not wrong, it's just a small piece of something

5
00:00:14,379 --> 00:00:14,790
bigger.

6
00:00:14,790 --> 00:00:18,665
Every real feature sends the model far more than your instruction.

7
00:00:18,665 --> 00:00:23,537
It sends retrieved documents, the last several messages of a conversation, examples

8
00:00:23,537 --> 00:00:28,292
of the format you want, results from tools it already called, and only then, your

9
00:00:28,292 --> 00:00:29,231
actual question.

10
00:00:29,231 --> 00:00:31,344
All of that together is the context.

11
00:00:31,344 --> 00:00:36,040
Managing it well is context engineering, and it's the actual skill behind almost

12
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:38,212
every AI feature that works reliably.

13
00:00:38,212 --> 00:00:40,090
The name change isn't marketing.

14
00:00:40,090 --> 00:00:44,728
It's an admission that the thing doing the work was never just the sentence you

15
00:00:44,728 --> 00:00:45,080
typed.

16
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:47,722
It also isn't just a rename for its own sake.

17
00:00:47,722 --> 00:00:52,535
When most people typed a prompt directly into a chat box, the prompt genuinely was

18
00:00:52,535 --> 00:00:56,116
almost the whole story, there wasn't much else in the window.

19
00:00:56,116 --> 00:01:00,929
The moment a feature started retrieving documents, remembering a conversation, and

20
00:01:00,929 --> 00:01:05,801
calling tools, most of what the model actually saw was being assembled by code, not

21
00:01:05,801 --> 00:01:10,732
typed by a person, and the old name stopped describing where the real decisions were

22
00:01:10,732 --> 00:01:11,378
being made.

23
00:01:11,928 --> 00:01:16,909
Here's roughly what's actually inside a real context window, in the order the pieces

24
00:01:16,909 --> 00:01:17,799
usually appear.

25
00:01:17,799 --> 00:01:22,780
System instructions come first: who the model is being asked to be, and the rules it

26
00:01:22,780 --> 00:01:23,610
should follow.

27
00:01:23,610 --> 00:01:27,642
Examples, if the feature uses them, show the shape of a good answer.

28
00:01:27,642 --> 00:01:32,564
Retrieved chunks are whatever your data layer pulled in for this specific question.

29
00:01:32,564 --> 00:01:36,300
Conversation history is everything said so far in this session.

30
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:40,807
Tool results are whatever came back from anything the system already called.

31
00:01:40,807 --> 00:01:45,373
And the user's actual message, the thing everyone assumes is "the prompt," is

32
00:01:45,373 --> 00:01:47,626
usually the smallest piece of the six.

33
00:01:47,626 --> 00:01:52,429
Context engineering is deciding what goes in each of those slots, how much of it,

34
00:01:52,429 --> 00:01:55,038
and in what order, for every single request.

35
00:01:55,038 --> 00:01:57,707
Prompt engineering was writing slot one well.

36
00:01:57,707 --> 00:01:59,308
It was never the whole job.

37
00:01:59,308 --> 00:02:03,222
Notice, too, that most of these slots change on every single call.

38
00:02:03,222 --> 00:02:05,594
Retrieved chunks depend on the question.

39
00:02:05,594 --> 00:02:07,076
History grows every turn.

40
00:02:07,076 --> 00:02:10,575
Tool results depend on what actually happened a moment ago.

41
00:02:10,575 --> 00:02:14,192
Only the system instructions and examples tend to stay fixed.

42
00:02:14,192 --> 00:02:19,173
That's the part a lot of PMs picture when they hear "the prompt," a document someone

43
00:02:19,173 --> 00:02:23,680
wrote once, when in practice it's a small, stable fraction of what the model

44
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:25,815
actually receives on any given call.

45
00:02:26,365 --> 00:02:29,136
Remember ticket 4471 from the tokens lecture.

46
00:02:29,136 --> 00:02:33,941
Here's the same instinct that caused that bug showing up again, in a different

47
00:02:33,941 --> 00:02:34,310
shape.

48
00:02:34,310 --> 00:02:38,868
The summary was wrong, and the first move was rewriting the system prompt.

49
00:02:38,868 --> 00:02:43,981
Four attempts, increasingly emphatic: be careful, double-check your work, make sure

50
00:02:43,981 --> 00:02:45,890
the summary matches the ticket.

51
00:02:45,890 --> 00:02:50,633
None of it changed the output, because none of it addressed what was actually

52
00:02:50,633 --> 00:02:51,249
happening.

53
00:02:51,249 --> 00:02:54,513
The real problem was sitting in the retrieved chunks.

54
00:02:54,513 --> 00:02:59,564
An old, resolved, near-duplicate ticket had been pulled in alongside the real one,

55
00:02:59,564 --> 00:03:03,198
and the model was accurately summarising a mixture of both.

56
00:03:03,198 --> 00:03:04,368
It wasn't confused.

57
00:03:04,368 --> 00:03:07,879
It was doing exactly what a mixed context asked it to do.

58
00:03:07,879 --> 00:03:12,992
The fix was in retrieval, not wording, and no version of a cleverer prompt was ever

59
00:03:12,992 --> 00:03:14,039
going to find it.

60
00:03:14,589 --> 00:03:19,637
Say the honest caveat, because "just add more context" is the opposite mistake, and

61
00:03:19,637 --> 00:03:22,009
it's just as common as under-including.

62
00:03:22,009 --> 00:03:27,118
Stuff the window with everything that might be relevant, and two things get worse at

63
00:03:27,118 --> 00:03:27,422
once.

64
00:03:27,422 --> 00:03:32,471
The model's attention isn't distributed evenly, it favours the start and the end of

65
00:03:32,471 --> 00:03:37,276
what it's given, so genuinely important material buried in the middle of a long

66
00:03:37,276 --> 00:03:39,830
context gets less weight than it deserves.

67
00:03:39,830 --> 00:03:44,879
And every token you add costs money and time, a conversation Section seven finishes

68
00:03:44,879 --> 00:03:45,426
properly.

69
00:03:45,426 --> 00:03:50,535
Context engineering means choosing what's in the window as carefully as you'd choose

70
00:03:50,535 --> 00:03:52,421
what's in an executive summary.

71
00:03:52,421 --> 00:03:53,880
Complete isn't the goal.

72
00:03:53,880 --> 00:03:54,732
Sufficient is.

73
00:03:55,282 --> 00:03:59,732
That's the definition worth carrying forward, and it explains why this lecture

74
00:03:59,732 --> 00:04:02,470
belongs in a course for PMs, not just engineers.

75
00:04:02,470 --> 00:04:06,920
You don't need to write the retrieval code to ask the right question about it.

76
00:04:06,920 --> 00:04:10,800
The exercise is the habit that matters most from this whole lecture.

77
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:15,592
Before you touch the prompt, look at the full context as it was actually sent, every

78
00:04:15,592 --> 00:04:16,448
slot, in order.

79
00:04:16,448 --> 00:04:20,327
More often than seems reasonable, the wording was never the problem.

80
00:04:20,877 --> 00:04:22,699
One thing before you move on.

81
00:04:22,699 --> 00:04:27,914
Find a real case where your feature gave a wrong answer, and get the actual context

82
00:04:27,914 --> 00:04:31,872
that was sent for that request, not the template, the real one.

83
00:04:31,872 --> 00:04:36,836
Read every slot, in order, and ask: which one did the problem actually live in?

84
00:04:36,836 --> 00:04:41,359
Instructions, examples, retrieval, history, tools, or truly the wording.

85
00:04:41,359 --> 00:04:43,998
Most of the time it won't be the last one.

86
00:04:43,998 --> 00:04:49,087
Next lecture takes the piece everyone underestimates, latency and throughput, and

87
00:04:49,087 --> 00:04:54,365
gives you the vocabulary to read a benchmark table the way an engineer already does.
